IgG-Mediated Food Sensitivities
Integrative & Functional medicine
Delayed onset systemic symptoms post-meals, Chronic abdominal bloating, Brain fog/fatigue, Elevated food-specific IgG antibodies

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IFM-019
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, debilitating brain fog, generalized intense body aching, and severe abdominal distension requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute bowel obstruction or acute central neurological event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing severe cognitive sluggishness (inability to complete sentences), diffuse severe joint pain, and profound abdominal distension. Urgent on-site physical and neurological assessment was strictly required to exclude an acute stroke, severe acute metabolic derangement, or acute bowel obstruction, preventing permanent neurological deficit or bowel ischemia prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My brain is completely full of cotton, I can't put a sentence together, and my stomach is painfully swollen like I'm pregnant."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a highly delayed, insidious onset of vague but severe systemic symptoms beginning roughly 24 hours after attending a large dinner party where they consumed multiple unaccustomed foods. The patient describes chronic, generalized distinct abdominal bloating and vague severe discomfort, which has acutely worsened today into painful distension. Bowel habits are currently completely irregular. The most distressing symptoms tonight are severe brain fog, prominent cognitive sluggishness, and significantly poor concentration; the spouse notes the patient has been staring blankly and struggling to hold a conversation. The patient also complains of profound generalized fatigue, a distinct heavy, sluggish feeling, and widespread vague migrating joint aching with distinct profound muscle stiffness. The patient reports a complete absence of immediate, life-threatening classic anaphylactic IgE-mediated allergic reactions (no hives or airway issues).
Functional Status: Patient is seated, appearing lethargic and mildly confused, unable to perform basic household tasks.
Social/Safety Concerns: Spouse is highly concerned about the sudden cognitive decline resembling an acute stroke or dementia flare.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/82 | HR: 78 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Patient appears physically uncomfortable, frequently rubbing their abdomen and forehead, with noticeably delayed verbal responses and blunted affect.
Physical Exam: Neurological exam reveals intact cranial nerves, normal symmetric motor strength, and normal reflexes. Crucially, while processing speed is slow (brain fog), orientation is intact and there is no expressive or receptive aphasia, focal weakness, or facial droop, ruling out an acute cerebrovascular event. Abdominal exam shows prominent generalized distension and mild, diffuse tympany to percussion, but the abdomen is entirely soft without localized tenderness, guarding, rigidity, or absent bowel sounds (normal active sounds present). Musculoskeletal exam shows diffuse, vague tenderness without localized joint effusion or severe erythema. The presentation fits the tetrad of delayed onset systemic symptoms post-meals, chronic abdominal bloating, and brain fog/fatigue. Confirmation anticipates elevated food-specific IgG antibodies on functional testing.
Environmental Observations: Patient is resting in a quiet living room. No obvious environmental toxins.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient occasionally takes over-the-counter NSAIDs for joint pain; no active prescription medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: IgG-Mediated Food Sensitivities - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Cerebrovascular Accident (Stroke/TIA) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of focal, lateralizing neurological signs (normal strength, sensation, and facial symmetry) and intact (though slow) speech.

Acute Mechanical Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal, non-tinkling bowel sounds, absence of severe colicky pain, and no history of intractable bilious vomiting.

Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) Flare - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of malar rash, fever, or frank, hot polyarthritis, though formal autoimmune serology would definitively exclude.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with acute, severe cognitive slowing (brain fog), significant abdominal distension, and diffuse systemic pain. Telephone triage correctly identified the emergent need to evaluate for an acute stroke (given the cognitive changes) or an acute surgical abdomen. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to perform a formal stroke screen (FAST assessment), auscultate the abdomen, and evaluate for peritonism. The physical exclusion of focal neurological deficits and a rigid, silent abdomen confirmed immediate medical stability. However, the severity of the cognitive impairment, the debilitating abdominal pain, and the immense anxiety of the spouse dictated that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed an unacceptable risk of profound psychological distress, potential diagnostic delay of a subtle ischemic event, and severe unmanaged pain. This urgent intervention unequivocally satisfies MBS Items 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided to the patient and spouse regarding the absence of an acute stroke or bowel obstruction. Advised a strict, simple whole-foods diet (e.g., plain rice, steamed vegetables) for 48 hours to reduce the immune burden. Recommend routine GP follow-up for specialized functional medicine blood assays (IgG food panel) and comprehensive inflammatory markers.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage chronic immune reactivity and bloating, initiate a strict elimination and reintroduction framework. Action: Implement a strict 14-day elimination of all gluten, dairy, and refined sugars. Consequence: Removing the most common, highly reactive dietary antigens physically halts the formation of IgG immune complexes, directly rewarding the patient with a clearance of the severe brain fog and migrating joint pain. Action: Maintain a detailed daily food and symptom diary. Consequence: Correlating delayed symptoms (24-72 hours post-meal) with specific intakes explicitly reveals the hidden trigger foods, empowering the patient to avoid future acute, debilitating systemic crashes.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated currently.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours to discuss dietary strategies and blood test requisitions.

